  description="China to Sweden freight guide: Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen to Gothenburg (SEGOT) with the east-coast Stockholm/Norvik split, roughly 32–40-day sea transit via the Cape plus ~1–2 days inland, 20–25-day rail via Hamburg/Duisburg, EU common duty, 25% VAT, SEK currency risk, EORI and hidden destination charges."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Shipping from China to Sweden' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping from China to Sweden: Gothenburg SEGOT gateway, 25% VAT, SEK currency &amp; the Stockholm split</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A one-page freight brief for importers moving cargo from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan, Shenzhen
        and the wider China origin set to Gothenburg and the Swedish market — with the Gothenburg-vs-
        Stockholm geography, the roughly 32–40-day sea window via the Cape plus an inland leg, the
        20–25-day rail alternative, EU common duty plus 25% VAT, the SEK currency risk, the EORI gate,
        and the hidden destination charges that shape the Swedish landed cost.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Sweden is entered through Gothenburg (SEGOT), the only Swedish port with mainline container capacity, but the biggest consumer market is Stockholm ~470 km east — so the inland leg decides the landed cost. The statutory math compounds: 5% duty + 25% VAT = 1.05 × 1.25 = <strong>×1.3125</strong>, so a $10,000 CIF becomes <strong>$13,125</strong> before inland and destination fees — <strong>$630 higher</strong> than Germany's 19% VAT on the same base. Rail lands in <strong>20–25 days</strong> total versus <strong>32–40 days</strong> sea plus ~1–2 days inland, at roughly 2–3× the sea rate. Sweden is also outside the eurozone, so you pay freight in USD and VAT in <strong>SEK</strong> — a double currency exposure.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Sweden's FCL/LCL
        benchmarks come from the WorldFreightHub Europe route data Rotterdam northern-range corridor
        (MEDIUM) and are labelled as reference only, while Gothenburg-specific, rail, air, express and
        DDP dollar figures were not published in the snapshot and are shown as request-for-quote markers.
        Treat every LOW-confidence figure as an indicative planning input and verify with your carrier
        before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Sweden import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">EU common duty 0–12% <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">25% <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Currency</span>
          <span class="fact-value">SEK <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
