    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Sea planning default:</strong> Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen to Gothenburg are
      recorded at roughly 32–40 days on the current Cape routing, plus ~1–2 days inland to Stockholm.
      Rail is roughly 20–25 days total via Hamburg/Duisburg. Both are planning estimates, not carrier
      promises — for air, express or a specific door-to-door move, request a routing quote before
      committing to a date. Note that China–Europe rail runs 1435 mm standard gauge → 1520 mm broad gauge
      → back to 1435 mm at the EU border, with transshipment/regauge at points such as Małaszewicze
      (Poland), before the Hamburg/Duisburg corridor serves Sweden across the Øresund crossing.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Sea vs rail vs air: Sweden's true door-to-door decision</h2>
      <p>
        In a country with its own deep-sea port you can compare a port-to-port sea quote with a rail
        quote — but Sweden's west-coast port is not its east-coast market. The honest comparison is
        therefore <strong>door-to-door, not port-to-port</strong>: roughly <strong>32–40 days</strong>
        by sea to Gothenburg plus <strong>~1–2 days</strong> inland to Stockholm, against roughly
        <strong>20–25 days</strong> by rail via the Hamburg/Duisburg corridor and the Øresund crossing.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode' },
          { key: 'bestFor', label: 'Best for' },
          { key: 'indicativeTime', label: 'Indicative time' },
          { key: 'indicativeCost', label: 'Indicative cost' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
